      drillAria: (c) => `Open ${c}`, failed: 'Failed to load',
      foot: 'Source: Japanese customs statistics (Ministry of Finance, Trade Statistics of Japan, e-Stat portal), monthly by 9-digit code: “commodity by country” tables from 2017 and “customs office by commodity by country” tables from 2021. Russia has not published customs statistics since 2022, so both sides of the trade come from Japanese data: Russian exports are Japan’s imports of goods of Russian origin (CIF), Japanese exports are shipments with final destination Russia (FOB). Yen are converted to US dollars at the monthly average rate (US Federal Reserve, series EXJPUS). Imports for the latest month are preliminary and are replaced by detailed figures a month later. The first 6 digits are the international HS code (HS2017 until 2021, HS2022 from 2022); the last 3 are Japan’s own detail and differ between exports and imports. Codes with “X” are kept confidential at a trader’s request (1–2 exporters or importers per code): the value is published, the exact product is hidden. Codes starting with “00” are re-exports, re-imports and aircraft stores. OEC draws similar charts from the BACI database (CEPII), which is built from the same data via UN Comtrade and updated once a year with a lag of about 18 months. Product names were translated for this dashboard. Map: Natural Earth.',
      leads: {
        lng: 'Raw materials under long-term contracts. Buyers of LNG, crude oil and coal are Japanese power and gas utilities and refiners; participants in Sakhalin-1 and Sakhalin-2 are disclosed in their annual reports. The port of arrival on the map points to a specific terminal and so to the buyer.',
        pgm: 'Precious metals usually travel by air (airport offices on the map). In Japan the buyers are trading houses, refiners and catalyst makers; in Russia, the metal producer and its traders.',
        metals: 'Metals are usually bought by Japanese trading houses and then go to foundries, rolling mills and auto-parts plants. The port of arrival on the map narrows the search to a region.',
        fish: 'Seafood: suppliers are Russian fishing companies holding quotas; the Federal Agency for Fishery publishes them when quotas are allocated. Buyers are importers, wholesalers and processors near the offices of arrival: live product usually goes to Hokkaido, frozen product to the large ports (map below).',
        wood: 'Timber: exporters can be found through deals in LesEGAIS, the Russian timber register, which lists the tax IDs of the parties. Buyers are timber traders and house-kit makers in the regions of the ports of arrival.',
        cars: 'Almost all car exports to Russia are used cars (visible at HS9 level). Exporters are Japanese companies buying at auctions, usually near the ports of shipment (map). Buyers are dealers and private individuals, mostly in the Russian Far East.',
        pharma: 'Medicines: the Russian State Register of Medicines lists the registration holders of Japanese drugs. Public procurement shows the end customers, hospitals, by country of origin “Japan”.',
        medical: 'Medical and measuring instruments: the Roszdravnadzor register of medical devices lists the manufacturer and its authorised representative in Russia. Public procurement shows the end customers.',
        equipment: 'Machinery, tyres, parts: the EAEU register of declarations and certificates of conformity (Rosaccreditation) shows the applicant, usually the importer, the Japanese manufacturer and the HS code. This is the most direct open way to find importers of specific brands.',
        consumer: 'Food ingredients, cosmetics, hygiene products: the EAEU state registration register and the Rosaccreditation register show the recipient (importer) and the manufacturer.',
      },
      links: { ais: 'Ship tracking (AIS)', fish: 'Federal Agency for Fishery', les: 'LesEGAIS', grls: 'State Register of Medicines', zakupki: 'Russian public procurement', med: 'Medical device register', fsa: 'Rosaccreditation register', sgr: 'EAEU state registration register' },
